menu "Audio"

config AUDIO_LOCAL_LOOPBACK_TEST
    bool "Enable temporary local mic-to-speaker loopback test"
    default n

config APP_AUDIO_AEC_ENABLE
    bool "Enable ESP-SR acoustic echo cancellation"
    default y
    help
        Preloads the ESP-SR AEC engine when a full-duplex audio owner prepares
        its media path. Runtime processing is enabled for IPC viewing, device
        calls, WeChat VoIP, and AI Chat, and is disabled outside those media
        sessions. Each application keeps its existing audio ownership model.

config APP_AUDIO_AEC_REF_DELAY_MS
    int "Speaker-to-microphone AEC reference delay (ms)"
    depends on APP_AUDIO_AEC_ENABLE
    range 40 160
    default 80
    help
        Compensates the codec, I2S DMA, amplifier, and acoustic path delay.
        Start at 80 ms for this board and adjust only from full-duplex echo
        tests; an incorrect value reduces cancellation quality.

endmenu

menu "WeChat VoIP"

config APP_WECHAT_VOIP_LOCAL_VIDEO_ENABLE
    bool "Upload local camera video during WeChat calls"
    default y
    help
        Advertise WeChat calls as video capable and proactively publish the
        ESP32-P4 camera through the existing YUV420-to-H264 RTC pipeline after
        CALL_CONNECTED. The WeChat audio path remains externally owned by the
        dedicated PCMA capture task.

endmenu

menu "RTC video uplink"

config APP_RTC_H264_DIRECT_HW_ENCODER
    bool "Use direct ESP_H264 hardware encoder for RTC video"
    default y
    help
        Bypass the V4L2 memory-to-memory H264 wrapper on the RTC camera
        uplink hot path and call the ESP32-P4 hardware encoder directly.
        The direct path keeps the native 1280x960 encoder output in PSRAM and leaves
        enough internal DMA memory for ESP-Hosted SDIO during full-duplex
        audio/video calls.

config APP_RTC_H264_RESOURCE_FALLBACK_ENABLE
    bool "Allow H264 resource-admission resolution fallback"
    default y
    help
        If the requested hardware encoder cannot reserve one contiguous
        internal reference-frame block, retry at the largest aligned size that
        fits the current heap. This is a local resource safety net only: it
        does not enable weak-network adaptation or change the saved RTC video
        policy. A normally prewarmed encoder still uses the requested size.

config APP_RTC_H264_BITRATE
    int "RTC H264 target bitrate"
    range 500000 12000000
    default 4000000
    help
        Target bitrate used by the ESP32-P4 hardware H264 encoder for the
        RTC camera uplink. The normal profile is tuned for sustained 1280x960 at 20fps
        with bounded send latency; raise this only after logs prove the
        transport keeps draining without backpressure.

config APP_RTC_H264_FPS
    int "RTC H264 target FPS"
    range 5 30
    default 20
    help
        Normal RTC camera uplink frame rate. Keep this separate from the
        camera sensor mode: the OV5647 uses its 1280x960 binning mode, while
        the current TiRTC demo target sends the native YUV420 frame at 20fps with bounded send
        latency. Weak-network adaptation remains disabled unless explicitly
        enabled below.

config APP_DEVICE_CALL_VIDEO_WIDTH
    int "Device-call H264 width"
    range 320 1280
    default 480
    help
        Video profile used only for bidirectional device calls. The default
        matches the 480x320 landscape display and asks the ISP for the same
        native YUV420 output, avoiding an application-side resize. IPC viewing
        keeps the normal RTC profile above.

config APP_DEVICE_CALL_VIDEO_HEIGHT
    int "Device-call H264 height"
    range 240 960
    default 320

config APP_DEVICE_CALL_VIDEO_FPS
    int "Device-call H264 FPS"
    range 5 30
    default 15
    help
        The full-duplex call path defaults to a stable 15fps. This is the
        minimum acceptance target under simultaneous encode, software decode,
        audio, UI and Wi-Fi load; higher profiles can be selected only after
        runtime statistics prove enough headroom.

config APP_DEVICE_CALL_VIDEO_BITRATE
    int "Device-call H264 target bitrate"
    range 500000 6000000
    default 2000000
    help
        Encoder target used only for P4-to-P4 video calls. It is restored to
        the previous RTC setting when the call ends.

config APP_DEVICE_CALL_VIDEO_MIN_QP
    int "Device-call H264 minimum QP"
    range 10 51
    default 26
    help
        Quality floor used only for the 480x320 bidirectional call profile.
        This is intentionally lower than the high-resolution IPC floor so a
        call can use its bitrate budget without changing IPC behavior.

config APP_DEVICE_CALL_VIDEO_MAX_QP
    int "Device-call H264 maximum QP"
    range 10 51
    default 38
    help
        Quality ceiling used only for the bidirectional call profile. Keep it
        greater than or equal to APP_DEVICE_CALL_VIDEO_MIN_QP.

config APP_CALL_VIDEO_DIRECT_LCD
    bool "Use PSRAM-direct LCD DMA for call video"
    depends on SOC_PSRAM_DMA_CAPABLE && SPIRAM
    default y
    help
        Send each decoded RGB565 call viewport to the SPI LCD as one direct
        PSRAM DMA transfer, bypassing LVGL image composition and internal frame
        bounce buffers. LVGL remains responsible for controls, touch input, and
        all non-video pages. Disable this only to compare with the slower LVGL
        image fallback path.

config APP_RTC_H264_GOP
    int "RTC H264 GOP"
    range 1 120
    default 40
    help
        GOP used by the ESP32-P4 hardware H264 encoder. Keep this separate
        from FPS: startup and peer recovery still request explicit key frames,
        while the normal periodic IDR interval should be long enough that the
        browser/proxy path does not see a visible 0.5s key-frame cadence.

config APP_RTC_H264_MIN_QP
    int "RTC H264 minimum QP"
    range 10 51
    default 34
    help
        Lower bound for encoder QP. Keep this high enough to prevent the
        ESP32-P4 H264 encoder from emitting very large startup delta frames
        that can overflow the proxy/browser path. This is not weak-network
        adaptation; the target bitrate remains configured separately.

config APP_RTC_H264_MAX_QP
    int "RTC H264 maximum QP"
    range 10 51
    default 45

config APP_RTC_H264_OUTPUT_BUFFER_BYTES
    int "RTC H264 output buffer bytes"
    range 524288 4194304
    default 1048576
    help
        MMAP output buffer size for one encoded H264 frame. Keep enough room
        for 1280x960 key frames and high-motion bursts. This buffer does not
        control adaptive downshift; it only protects the encoder output path.

config APP_RTC_H264_MAX_DELTA_PAYLOAD_BYTES
    int "RTC H264 maximum delta frame payload bytes"
    range 65536 1048576
    default 262144
    help
        Hard guard for abnormal non-key frame payloads. Normal operation must
        not drop H264 P frames after encoding, because that breaks the
        reference chain and can make the browser/proxy show a short recovery
        hiccup. Keep the steady-state guard high enough for normal 1280x960 motion
        detail, while the startup guard below remains tighter during decoder
        warmup. GOP should remain short so recovery follows quickly.

config APP_RTC_H264_STARTUP_GUARD_MS
    int "RTC H264 startup transport guard window in milliseconds"
    range 0 10000
    default 2500
    help
        Startup-only window that uses a tighter delta-frame burst limit while
        the browser/proxy decoder and data channels are still warming up. This
        is not weak-network adaptation; after the window expires the normal
        max-delta policy above applies.

config APP_RTC_H264_STARTUP_MAX_DELTA_PAYLOAD_BYTES
    int "RTC H264 startup maximum delta frame payload bytes"
    range 65536 1048576
    default 131072
    help
        Delta-frame payload limit used only during the startup guard window.
        This defaults to the normal max-delta guard so startup uses the same
        burst protection as steady-state operation.

config APP_RTC_VIDEO_AUTO_ADAPT_ENABLE
    bool "Enable automatic RTC video weak-network adaptation"
    default n
    help
        Keep this disabled for the normal demo path: the device will try to
        output the configured full frame rate, resolution, and bitrate.
        When enabled, future TiRTC/network weak-link callbacks may call the
        auto adaptation entry to lower frame rate or resolution through the
        media governor policy table.

config APP_RTC_WAIT_VIDEO_SUBSCRIBE_BEFORE_CAPTURE
    bool "Start camera capture after remote video subscription"
    default n
    help
        Disable this for the normal IPC demo path so the device starts the
        physical camera/H264 hot path as soon as a call is accepted. That keeps
        the capture/encoder path warm before the WebRTC proxy sends its video
        subscription and shortens first-frame latency.

        Enable it only when saving camera/H264 work before a confirmed remote
        subscription is more important than first-frame time.

config APP_DEVICE_ONLINE_PAUSE_DURING_RTC
    bool "Pause device-online MQTT while RTC media is active"
    default n
    help
        Keep this disabled for normal operation. The permanent MQTT connection
        carries call_cancel, unbind, callers_update and other business events
        that must remain available while RTC media is active.

        Enable this only for isolated media stress tests where losing realtime
        business notifications during the call is acceptable.

config APP_DMA_ESCROW_BYTES
    int "Application DMA escrow bytes"
    range 0 131072
    default 65536
    help
        Internal DMA-capable memory held by the media layer at early boot.
        The H264 prewarm path can release this escrow and retry encoder open
        if the hardware encoder cannot acquire enough internal reference
        memory after the standard IDF DMA reserve has been created.

config APP_CAMERA_FRAME_TRACE_INITIAL_COUNT
    int "Camera frame trace initial frame count"
    range 0 64
    default 0
    help
        Number of per-frame camera pipeline trace lines printed after a video
        session starts. Keep this at zero for performance tuning; the periodic
        aggregate stats remain enabled and are enough for FPS/bitrate analysis.

config APP_CAMERA_FRAME_TRACE_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Camera slow/large frame trace interval in milliseconds"
    range 200 60000
    default 10000
    help
        Minimum interval for slow or large frame trace logs. This prevents
        diagnostic logging from disturbing the 1280x960 H264 hot path while still
        keeping enough evidence when the encoder or transport stalls.

config APP_RTC_H264_KEY_FRAME_REQUEST_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Minimum interval between local H264 key-frame requests"
    range 0 5000
    default 2000
    help
        Coalesce repeated peer-side IDR requests against the last emitted IDR.
        The normal 40-frame GOP at 20fps already emits an IDR every two
        seconds. Reconfiguring the hardware encoder more often can reset GOP
        progress and stall the bidirectional media path. Stream start still
        explicitly requests the first key frame.

config APP_RTC_TRANSPORT_BACKPRESSURE_HOLD_MS
    int "RTC transport backpressure hold in milliseconds"
    range 40 1000
    default 300
    help
        Short camera hold window after the TiRTC send buffer crosses the
        transport guard waterline. This is not weak-network adaptation and
        does not change the configured native-resolution/bitrate profile; it only prevents
        the SDK send buffer from filling so far that heartbeat/control traffic
        times out.

config APP_TIRTC_QUERY_SEND_BUFFER_USED
    bool "Query TiRTC SDK send-buffer usage"
    default y
    help
        Keep SDK send-buffer backpressure enabled on the send hot path.
        Close/error snapshots still use the cached waterline only, because
        some SDK builds can invalidate a connection handle during remote close.
        Disable this only when isolating SDK send-buffer internals.

endmenu

menu "Debug utilities"

config APP_VERBOSE_RUNTIME_LOGS
    bool "Enable verbose runtime flow logs"
    default n
    help
        Restore detailed HTTP, MQTT, ESP-Hosted, media handoff, and call-state
        tracing for a focused diagnosis. Normal builds keep lifecycle, first
        frame, warning, and error logs while suppressing repetitive internals.

config APP_MEDIA_PERIODIC_DIAGNOSTICS
    bool "Enable periodic media performance diagnostics"
    default n
    help
        Print long camera, RTC transmit, decoder, audio-level, and whole-system
        snapshots while media is running. These lines are useful for a focused
        profiling session, but synchronous console output can pause realtime
        media tasks and create visible periodic video stalls. Keep this off for
        normal calls and enable it only while collecting performance evidence.

config APP_DEBUG_SCREEN_SERVER_ENABLE
    bool "Enable LAN screen debug web server"
    default n
    help
        Starts a local HTTP server after the display is initialized.
        The server exposes the current LVGL screen as BMP and accepts
        debug tap/scroll commands from a browser on the same Wi-Fi LAN.
        Disable this for production builds to avoid compiling or linking
        the HTTP debug server.

config APP_DEBUG_SCREEN_SERVER_PORT
    int "LAN screen debug web server port"
    range 1024 65534
    default 8080
    depends on APP_DEBUG_SCREEN_SERVER_ENABLE

endmenu
